<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Denis Rapontchombo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1780–1876</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as King Denis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Denis Rapontchombo, born Antchouwé Kowe Rapontchombo and known to Europeans as King Denis, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpongwe">Mpongwe</a> ruler on the south bank of the Gabon estuary. In 1839 he signed a treaty with the French naval officer Édouard Bouët-Willaumez granting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rights on part of the estuary, the first step towards the colony of Gabon. He was known as a skilled trader and intermediary with European merchants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denis-rapontchombo.jpg" alt="Denis Rapontchombo" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D._Dinis_-_Compendio_de_cr%C3%B3nicas_de_reyes_(Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
